1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,250
Throw a stick onto a wooden floor.

2
00:00:03,750 --> 00:00:05,710
Then another, and another.

3
00:00:06,690 --> 00:00:10,290
Count how often one lands across
a crack between the boards,

4
00:00:10,340 --> 00:00:13,300
and from that count alone,
you can work out pi.

5
00:00:15,300 --> 00:00:18,220
There is no circle anywhere on this floor.

6
00:00:18,880 --> 00:00:20,510
So where is it hiding?

7
00:00:22,850 --> 00:00:25,680
Most of us met math as a list of chores.

8
00:00:26,170 --> 00:00:28,720
Add these.
Solve for x.

9
00:00:28,850 --> 00:00:30,030
Show your work.

10
00:00:31,230 --> 00:00:33,850
Mathematicians mean
something else by the word.

11
00:00:34,140 --> 00:00:36,790
They mean the study of patterns.

12
00:00:37,110 --> 00:00:38,640
A ripple on a pond,

13
00:00:38,690 --> 00:00:43,010
the rim of a wheel and the face
of a clock all share one shape.

14
00:00:43,130 --> 00:00:48,000
Math studies that shape stripped down,
until only the pattern is left.

15
00:00:50,050 --> 00:00:53,390
In school,
math arrives one piece at a time.

16
00:00:53,760 --> 00:00:58,620
A year of algebra, a year of geometry,
a little statistics near the end.

17
00:00:59,750 --> 00:01:04,130
Each piece makes sense on its own,
but nobody shows how they fit together.

18
00:01:04,950 --> 00:01:09,390
It's like learning a city one street
at a time, without ever seeing the map.

19
00:01:10,470 --> 00:01:14,270
With the map,
a new idea stops being a stray fact.

20
00:01:14,520 --> 00:01:18,380
It becomes a place you can find again,
next to things you already know.

21
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:23,950
And the map is worth seeing,
because its pieces are everywhere.

22
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:27,170
Your phone finds you where
circles of distance around

23
00:01:27,220 --> 00:01:28,900
several satellites meet.

24
00:01:29,410 --> 00:01:32,890
A spam filter weighs the odds
that a message is junk.

25
00:01:33,480 --> 00:01:37,370
Every secure payment leans
on the arithmetic of whole numbers.

26
00:01:37,950 --> 00:01:42,500
And spacecraft reach other planets
because calculus tells us where to aim.

27
00:01:43,550 --> 00:01:45,240
So, the plan.

28
00:01:45,450 --> 00:01:50,420
We'll take one circle and look at it
through each major branch of math in turn:

29
00:01:50,640 --> 00:01:54,010
what it is, how big it is, how it turns,

30
00:01:54,060 --> 00:01:56,930
what shape it really has, how it grows,

31
00:01:56,980 --> 00:01:59,190
and what it has to do with chance.

32
00:02:00,620 --> 00:02:03,210
Each look lights up one part of the map.

33
00:02:04,850 --> 00:02:07,210
Before we start, a promise.

34
00:02:07,630 --> 00:02:12,037
By the end, you'll see exactly why
those sticks on the floor found pi,

35
00:02:12,087 --> 00:02:15,490
and why the answer needs every
part of this map at once.

36
00:02:16,480 --> 00:02:18,600
And one small thing to watch for.

37
00:02:19,050 --> 00:02:23,030
Each time we move on, the whole
map will turn by the same amount.

38
00:02:23,480 --> 00:02:26,640
That turning is math too,
and we'll come back to it.

39
00:02:28,120 --> 00:02:31,560
We start where all of math starts:
with a definition.

40
00:02:32,270 --> 00:02:34,730
What, exactly, is a circle?

41
00:02:36,340 --> 00:02:38,580
Picture a dog tied to a post.

42
00:02:38,890 --> 00:02:40,580
When it pulls the leash tight,

43
00:02:40,630 --> 00:02:44,180
it can stand in lots of places,
but not just anywhere.

44
00:02:45,050 --> 00:02:46,270
Every spot it can reach

45
00:02:46,320 --> 00:02:50,290
with the leash tight is exactly
one leash length from the post.

46
00:02:51,510 --> 00:02:54,500
Gather up all of those spots,
and they make a circle.

47
00:02:55,530 --> 00:02:57,350
That shift, from a shape we

48
00:02:57,400 --> 00:03:01,940
recognize to a rule we can check,
is the first idea of foundations.

49
00:03:02,990 --> 00:03:06,270
Mathematicians call
a collection like this a set.

50
00:03:06,790 --> 00:03:10,110
A set is defined by a rule,
and the rule decides,

51
00:03:10,160 --> 00:03:13,390
for every possible point,
whether it's in or out.

52
00:03:14,330 --> 00:03:15,850
There's no partly in.

53
00:03:16,610 --> 00:03:19,010
Change the rule, and you change the set.

54
00:03:19,450 --> 00:03:23,270
Let the leash go slack,
and the set fills in to a whole disk.

55
00:03:24,290 --> 00:03:28,290
The circle is only the points
that are exactly one leash length away.

56
00:03:29,330 --> 00:03:33,350
To make the rule exact,
put the post at the center of a grid.

57
00:03:34,020 --> 00:03:35,940
Every point on the circle is the corner

58
00:03:35,980 --> 00:03:39,200
of a right triangle whose
longest side is the leash.

59
00:03:39,610 --> 00:03:41,190
Pythagoras turns one leash

60
00:03:41,240 --> 00:03:45,130
length away into a test you can
run on any point's coordinates.

61
00:03:46,360 --> 00:03:50,180
Wrap that test in braces,
and the circle is written as a set.

62
00:03:50,790 --> 00:03:55,090
Georg Cantor started the study of sets
in the eighteen seventies, and sets

63
00:03:55,140 --> 00:03:57,520
became the ground floor of mathematics.

64
00:03:58,140 --> 00:04:02,520
A point belongs to this one
if and only if it passes the test.

65
00:04:02,950 --> 00:04:05,350
The promise runs in both directions.

66
00:04:06,490 --> 00:04:09,430
Try it with a leash five units long.

67
00:04:09,480 --> 00:04:12,600
Which of these points can the dog
reach with the leash tight?

68
00:04:14,830 --> 00:04:18,880
The first passes the test exactly,
so it sits on the circle.

69
00:04:19,660 --> 00:04:23,010
The second comes up short, so it's inside.

70
00:04:23,730 --> 00:04:26,400
The third overshoots: outside.

71
00:04:27,530 --> 00:04:32,300
One test sorts every point in the plane
into exactly one of three sets,

72
00:04:32,340 --> 00:04:35,270
with nothing left over
and nothing counted twice.

73
00:04:36,430 --> 00:04:38,850
That two-way promise matters.

74
00:04:39,590 --> 00:04:41,747
Roll a circle under a flat board,

75
00:04:41,773 --> 00:04:44,890
and the board glides along
at a constant height.

76
00:04:45,320 --> 00:04:50,560
So you might guess the reverse: anything
that rolls that smoothly must be a circle.

77
00:04:52,030 --> 00:04:53,300
It doesn't have to be.

78
00:04:53,680 --> 00:04:56,690
This rounded triangle,
built from three arcs,

79
00:04:56,740 --> 00:04:58,700
keeps the board just as level.

80
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:04,020
The British fifty pence coin uses
the same trick with seven sides,

81
00:05:04,060 --> 00:05:07,150
so vending machines can measure
its width like a circle's.

82
00:05:08,310 --> 00:05:11,110
A statement and its reverse
are different claims,

83
00:05:11,160 --> 00:05:13,780
and logic is the habit of checking both.

84
00:05:15,040 --> 00:05:17,490
Anyone who shops online uses this.

85
00:05:17,960 --> 00:05:21,310
Filter for shoes that are red
and in your size, and the shop

86
00:05:21,360 --> 00:05:23,640
returns the overlap of two sets.

87
00:05:24,350 --> 00:05:28,810
Ask for red or your size,
and it returns everything in either one.

88
00:05:30,090 --> 00:05:34,940
Underneath all of math sit definitions
like these: rules that say exactly what

89
00:05:34,980 --> 00:05:36,710
is in and what is out.

90
00:05:37,750 --> 00:05:39,850
Everything else is built on top.

91
00:05:43,040 --> 00:05:44,660
How big is a circle?

92
00:05:45,410 --> 00:05:50,330
Take a wheel exactly one unit across,
paint a dot on its rim, and roll it

93
00:05:50,380 --> 00:05:52,830
along a number line for one full turn.

94
00:05:53,350 --> 00:05:55,060
Where will the dot touch down?

95
00:05:57,310 --> 00:05:59,520
Just past three.

96
00:05:59,760 --> 00:06:04,100
That length is the distance around
the wheel, and it has a name: pi.

97
00:06:05,220 --> 00:06:08,430
Every circle,
from a coin to a Ferris wheel, measures

98
00:06:08,480 --> 00:06:11,160
pi times its own width all the way around.

99
00:06:12,270 --> 00:06:14,900
So pi is a little more than three.

100
00:06:15,150 --> 00:06:20,150
You'd expect some fraction to land on it
exactly, if we just find the right one.

101
00:06:21,240 --> 00:06:25,370
More than two thousand years ago,
Archimedes trapped pi between two

102
00:06:25,420 --> 00:06:28,970
fractions by drawing polygons
with ninety-six sides.

103
00:06:29,660 --> 00:06:33,520
The upper one, twenty-two sevenths,
is still used in schools.

104
00:06:34,270 --> 00:06:38,400
Around the year four eighty,
Zu Chongzhi in China found three hundred

105
00:06:38,440 --> 00:06:43,360
fifty-five over one hundred thirteen,
right to six decimal places.

106
00:06:43,990 --> 00:06:46,910
Zoom in on either one,
and there is still a gap.

107
00:06:47,980 --> 00:06:50,480
Each kind of number answers a need.

108
00:06:50,850 --> 00:06:52,880
Counting gives the whole numbers.

109
00:06:53,240 --> 00:06:55,020
Owing gives the negatives.

110
00:06:55,430 --> 00:06:59,980
Sharing a pizza gives fractions, which
mathematicians call rational numbers.

111
00:07:00,900 --> 00:07:06,167
And fractions are packed tight: between
any two sits another, halfway between,

112
00:07:06,193 --> 00:07:08,830
and another between those, without end.

113
00:07:09,890 --> 00:07:12,850
It looks as if they leave
no room for anything else.

114
00:07:13,790 --> 00:07:16,940
Fit a square inside
a circle of radius one.

115
00:07:17,560 --> 00:07:21,820
Its diagonal runs straight across
the circle, so it's two units long,

116
00:07:21,870 --> 00:07:25,430
and Pythagoras makes each
side the square root of two.

117
00:07:26,490 --> 00:07:28,110
Is that length a fraction?

118
00:07:29,740 --> 00:07:32,120
Suppose it is, in lowest terms.

119
00:07:32,460 --> 00:07:35,550
Squaring gives an equation
in whole numbers,

120
00:07:35,600 --> 00:07:38,070
and it forces the top number to be even.

121
00:07:38,910 --> 00:07:43,000
Feed that back in,
and the bottom number must be even too.

122
00:07:43,400 --> 00:07:46,900
But a fraction in lowest terms
can't have both numbers even.

123
00:07:47,610 --> 00:07:52,800
The assumption fails, so the square
root of two is not a fraction.

124
00:07:52,900 --> 00:07:55,200
Numbers like this are called irrational.

125
00:07:56,420 --> 00:07:59,970
In seventeen sixty-one,
Johann Lambert proved that pi

126
00:08:00,020 --> 00:08:04,760
is irrational too, so its decimals
never fall into a repeating loop.

127
00:08:05,740 --> 00:08:08,230
In practice, we never need them all.

128
00:08:08,530 --> 00:08:11,550
Navigators at NASA use
fifteen decimal places

129
00:08:11,600 --> 00:08:14,180
to steer spacecraft between planets.

130
00:08:14,660 --> 00:08:16,470
Forty places would give the distance

131
00:08:16,520 --> 00:08:20,150
around the visible universe
to within the width of an atom.

132
00:08:21,430 --> 00:08:24,830
Numbers come in kinds,
and even a simple circle

133
00:08:24,880 --> 00:08:27,340
holds lengths that no fraction can name.

134
00:08:27,990 --> 00:08:29,800
And notice how we showed it:

135
00:08:30,050 --> 00:08:34,600
assume the opposite, follow the rules,
and arrive at something impossible.

136
00:08:35,280 --> 00:08:37,870
That move comes straight from foundations.

137
00:08:40,940 --> 00:08:44,020
Turn a square a little,
and you can tell it moved.

138
00:08:44,660 --> 00:08:49,220
Turn it a quarter of the way around,
and it looks exactly as it did before.

139
00:08:50,040 --> 00:08:51,410
Now turn the circle.

140
00:08:51,550 --> 00:08:55,410
Turn it by any amount at all,
and nothing seems to change.

141
00:08:56,270 --> 00:09:00,380
A move that leaves a shape looking
the same is called a symmetry.

142
00:09:00,670 --> 00:09:03,420
The square has four turning symmetries.

143
00:09:03,690 --> 00:09:06,060
The circle has one for every angle.

144
00:09:06,990 --> 00:09:09,870
Mathematicians study the moves themselves.

145
00:09:10,100 --> 00:09:12,470
Think of the dial on a combination lock.

146
00:09:12,860 --> 00:09:14,310
Turn it thirty degrees,

147
00:09:14,360 --> 00:09:18,890
then forty-five more, and together
that's one turn of seventy-five.

148
00:09:19,690 --> 00:09:22,260
Any turn can be undone by turning back,

149
00:09:22,300 --> 00:09:25,490
and leaving the dial alone
counts as a turn of zero.

150
00:09:26,640 --> 00:09:30,540
Moves you can combine and undo,
including the move that does nothing,

151
00:09:30,580 --> 00:09:32,370
form what's called a group.

152
00:09:32,750 --> 00:09:35,750
Groups are one of the basic
objects of algebra.

153
00:09:36,740 --> 00:09:39,610
Remember how the map turns
each time we move on?

154
00:09:39,960 --> 00:09:42,680
It's always a sixth of a full circle,

155
00:09:42,720 --> 00:09:45,700
and six of those bring
it back to the start.

156
00:09:46,300 --> 00:09:49,240
Those six turns,
counting the one that does nothing,

157
00:09:49,290 --> 00:09:53,230
form a group of their own,
the cyclic group of order six.

158
00:09:53,830 --> 00:09:56,550
A snowflake has the same six turns.

159
00:09:57,180 --> 00:10:00,760
By the end of this video,
the map will have made all six.

160
00:10:01,990 --> 00:10:03,910
Turns can be numbers too.

161
00:10:04,560 --> 00:10:09,190
Multiplying by minus one flips
the number line around: a half turn.

162
00:10:10,070 --> 00:10:12,210
So what would a quarter turn be?

163
00:10:13,570 --> 00:10:14,760
Call it i.

164
00:10:15,390 --> 00:10:21,060
Do it twice, and you've made a half turn,
the same as multiplying by minus one.

165
00:10:21,200 --> 00:10:24,770
That's all the square root
of minus one means: a quarter turn,

166
00:10:24,820 --> 00:10:26,980
off the line and into the plane.

167
00:10:28,220 --> 00:10:31,980
Leonhard Euler found a way
to write any turn as a number.

168
00:10:32,650 --> 00:10:37,400
Each one is a power of a single constant,
the number e, which we'll meet properly

169
00:10:37,440 --> 00:10:39,090
in the chapter on change.

170
00:10:40,340 --> 00:10:43,720
As the angle grows,
the point sweeps around the circle,

171
00:10:43,760 --> 00:10:48,120
with cosine as its shadow along
one axis and sine along the other.

172
00:10:48,820 --> 00:10:51,840
Keep an eye on e, i and pi.

173
00:10:52,130 --> 00:10:55,850
Before the end,
all three will meet in one short line.

174
00:10:57,030 --> 00:10:59,830
Turns written this way
are easy to combine.

175
00:11:00,390 --> 00:11:03,390
Take a turn of sixty degrees
and a turn of thirty.

176
00:11:03,890 --> 00:11:05,690
What does multiplying them give?

177
00:11:07,350 --> 00:11:10,250
Expanding the brackets,
the pieces combine,

178
00:11:10,300 --> 00:11:14,790
and everything cancels except i:
a turn of ninety degrees.

179
00:11:15,730 --> 00:11:18,340
Multiplying turns adds their angles.

180
00:11:18,740 --> 00:11:20,320
The angle formulas from school

181
00:11:20,360 --> 00:11:23,600
trigonometry are hiding
inside that one line.

182
00:11:24,810 --> 00:11:27,260
The same idea steers machines.

183
00:11:27,450 --> 00:11:32,210
In eighteen forty-three, William Rowan
Hamilton found numbers that do for turns

184
00:11:32,260 --> 00:11:38,030
in space what i does in the plane, and he
carved the rule into a bridge in Dublin.

185
00:11:38,570 --> 00:11:41,380
Today these numbers, called quaternions,

186
00:11:41,420 --> 00:11:45,140
turn the camera in video games
and tell your phone which way is up.

187
00:11:46,430 --> 00:11:51,020
For a circle, the moves are turns,
and the turns are numbers.

188
00:11:51,770 --> 00:11:54,460
Algebra is the study of moves like these.

189
00:11:57,680 --> 00:12:01,490
We're halfway around the map, and we
still owe you the sticks on the floor.

190
00:12:01,870 --> 00:12:03,150
We need a closer look

191
00:12:03,200 --> 00:12:07,170
at shape and at change first,
and then we'll have everything.

192
00:12:08,370 --> 00:12:09,890
So, shape.

193
00:12:10,360 --> 00:12:14,380
Think of the circle as a rubber band,
and squash it into a square.

194
00:12:15,830 --> 00:12:20,950
To a geometer, everything just changed:
the curve is gone, and corners appeared.

195
00:12:21,820 --> 00:12:24,460
To a topologist, nothing happened at all.

196
00:12:25,540 --> 00:12:29,080
Topology is geometry
with the measurements thrown away.

197
00:12:29,390 --> 00:12:34,080
Stretching, bending and squashing
are allowed, as much as you like.

198
00:12:34,590 --> 00:12:36,720
Cutting and gluing are not.

199
00:12:37,690 --> 00:12:42,950
So the rubber band can become a triangle,
or a wobbly blob, or a star.

200
00:12:43,450 --> 00:12:46,123
What it can't become is a figure eight,

201
00:12:46,173 --> 00:12:49,210
because that means pinching
two points together.

202
00:12:50,020 --> 00:12:53,900
To a topologist, every one
of these loops is the same circle.

203
00:12:55,030 --> 00:12:57,170
So what does survive a stretch?

204
00:12:57,960 --> 00:12:58,970
Holes.

205
00:12:59,550 --> 00:13:03,800
Draw a loop on a flat disk,
and it can always shrink down to a point.

206
00:13:04,490 --> 00:13:06,767
Wrap a loop once around the rubber band,

207
00:13:06,793 --> 00:13:09,710
and it snags on the hole,
however it's stretched.

208
00:13:10,710 --> 00:13:14,650
On the surface of a donut,
a loop can snag in two different ways.

209
00:13:15,850 --> 00:13:18,410
Loops that can't be shrunk
are one of the main ways

210
00:13:18,460 --> 00:13:20,520
topologists tell shapes apart.

211
00:13:21,650 --> 00:13:24,240
Euler found an even simpler test.

212
00:13:24,440 --> 00:13:27,860
Build a shape from corners,
edges and faces,

213
00:13:27,910 --> 00:13:31,140
add up the corners and faces,
and take away the edges.

214
00:13:32,150 --> 00:13:34,210
Any loop comes out at zero.

215
00:13:34,570 --> 00:13:37,440
Fill it in, and the face makes it one.

216
00:13:37,920 --> 00:13:39,890
A cube comes out at two.

217
00:13:40,770 --> 00:13:41,920
What about the donut?

218
00:13:43,670 --> 00:13:45,120
Zero again.

219
00:13:45,470 --> 00:13:47,920
Cut any of these into different pieces,

220
00:13:47,960 --> 00:13:51,630
or stretch them however you like,
and the count never changes.

221
00:13:52,930 --> 00:13:57,280
That's why people joke that a topologist
can't tell a coffee mug from a donut.

222
00:13:57,940 --> 00:14:02,770
Squash the mug down into its handle,
and what's left is a ring with one hole.

223
00:14:04,070 --> 00:14:05,990
Both count out at zero.

224
00:14:06,500 --> 00:14:08,760
To topology they're the same shape.

225
00:14:09,740 --> 00:14:14,770
Biologists use the same way of thinking
to follow how long strands of DNA tangle

226
00:14:14,820 --> 00:14:16,790
and untangle inside a cell.

227
00:14:17,880 --> 00:14:20,800
If you've ridden a subway,
you've used this idea.

228
00:14:21,350 --> 00:14:24,320
In nineteen thirty-three,
Harry Beck redrew the map

229
00:14:24,360 --> 00:14:28,740
of the London Underground with straight
lines and evenly spaced stops.

230
00:14:29,560 --> 00:14:33,510
Distances were wrong everywhere,
but which stops connect to which

231
00:14:33,560 --> 00:14:35,130
stayed exactly right.

232
00:14:36,280 --> 00:14:40,270
Riders found it far easier to use,
and transit maps around the world

233
00:14:40,320 --> 00:14:42,490
have copied the idea ever since.

234
00:14:43,660 --> 00:14:47,710
A circle and a square disagree
on every measurement, and agree

235
00:14:47,760 --> 00:14:50,080
on everything that survives a stretch.

236
00:14:50,980 --> 00:14:54,990
Even with every length thrown away,
we still ended up counting.

237
00:14:55,630 --> 00:15:00,000
Next, the lengths come back,
because the circle is about to grow.

238
00:15:03,200 --> 00:15:05,560
Drop a stone into still water.

239
00:15:06,830 --> 00:15:11,380
The ripple spreads as a circle,
and its radius grows at a steady pace.

240
00:15:12,260 --> 00:15:15,430
You'd expect the area
inside to grow steadily too.

241
00:15:16,730 --> 00:15:17,800
It doesn't.

242
00:15:18,390 --> 00:15:22,940
Every second, the ripple sweeps over
more new water than the second before.

243
00:15:24,040 --> 00:15:26,287
Asking how fast something is changing,

244
00:15:26,313 --> 00:15:29,980
at a single instant,
is the question behind calculus.

245
00:15:31,030 --> 00:15:33,920
A car's speedometer answers
that kind of question.

246
00:15:34,280 --> 00:15:36,880
It doesn't show
your average over the trip,

247
00:15:36,930 --> 00:15:39,280
but how fast you're going right now.

248
00:15:40,300 --> 00:15:42,240
To make right now precise,

249
00:15:42,290 --> 00:15:45,950
zoom in on a smooth curve until
it looks like a straight line.

250
00:15:46,490 --> 00:15:49,420
The steepness of that line
is the derivative:

251
00:15:49,470 --> 00:15:51,800
the rate of change at a single point.

252
00:15:53,040 --> 00:15:54,970
Isaac Newton and Gottfried Leibniz

253
00:15:55,020 --> 00:15:58,510
built calculus on this idea
in the late sixteen hundreds.

254
00:15:59,630 --> 00:16:05,628
One curve has a special property: at every
point, its steepness matches its height.

255
00:16:06,440 --> 00:16:08,090
It grows the way savings

256
00:16:08,140 --> 00:16:12,690
do when interest keeps compounding:
the more there is, the faster it grows.

257
00:16:13,170 --> 00:16:16,400
Its heights are powers
of one particular number,

258
00:16:16,450 --> 00:16:19,640
about two point seven one eight, called e.

259
00:16:20,550 --> 00:16:24,380
This is the same e that Euler
used to describe turns.

260
00:16:25,350 --> 00:16:26,670
Back to the circle.

261
00:16:27,150 --> 00:16:29,507
Grow its radius by a tiny amount,

262
00:16:29,533 --> 00:16:32,540
and the new area is a thin
ring around the edge.

263
00:16:33,270 --> 00:16:36,630
Cut the ring and unroll it,
and it's almost a rectangle:

264
00:16:36,710 --> 00:16:39,950
as long as the circumference,
and as thick as the nudge.

265
00:16:41,120 --> 00:16:44,880
So the area grows at a rate
equal to the length of the edge.

266
00:16:45,530 --> 00:16:48,800
The derivative of the circle's
area is its circumference.

267
00:16:49,750 --> 00:16:52,770
Try it on a pizza ten
centimeters in radius.

268
00:16:52,910 --> 00:16:56,640
Stretch the dough until the radius
grows by one millimeter.

269
00:16:57,080 --> 00:16:58,620
How much more pizza is that?

270
00:17:00,400 --> 00:17:05,000
The edge times the nudge says about
six point two eight square centimeters.

271
00:17:05,430 --> 00:17:08,930
Working it out exactly
gives six point three one.

272
00:17:09,600 --> 00:17:12,500
The small difference is there
because the ring's outer edge

273
00:17:12,540 --> 00:17:15,433
is a little longer than its inner edge.

274
00:17:15,587 --> 00:17:17,347
Make the nudge ten times smaller,

275
00:17:17,373 --> 00:17:19,870
and the difference gets
a hundred times smaller.

276
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:23,350
Calculus also runs the other way.

277
00:17:23,670 --> 00:17:29,668
Peel a whole disk into thin rings, unroll
each one, and stack them, smallest on top.

278
00:17:30,850 --> 00:17:32,190
They make a triangle

279
00:17:32,240 --> 00:17:35,720
as tall as the radius,
with the circumference as its base.

280
00:17:36,200 --> 00:17:40,010
Its area works out to exactly
the area of the circle.

281
00:17:40,710 --> 00:17:42,750
Adding up infinitely many thin

282
00:17:42,800 --> 00:17:46,650
pieces is called integration,
and it undoes the derivative.

283
00:17:47,510 --> 00:17:50,470
Archimedes proved the circle
matches this triangle more

284
00:17:50,520 --> 00:17:52,440
than two thousand years ago.

285
00:17:53,580 --> 00:17:56,140
Every weather forecast is built this way:

286
00:17:56,260 --> 00:18:01,030
small changes in air and water,
worked out step by step and added up.

287
00:18:02,110 --> 00:18:06,460
For a circle, the rate at which
its area grows is its own edge.

288
00:18:07,180 --> 00:18:11,310
Change is measured by derivatives
and added up by integrals.

289
00:18:14,490 --> 00:18:17,500
Back to the floor and the sticks,
as promised.

290
00:18:18,450 --> 00:18:20,580
One throw is unpredictable.

291
00:18:21,100 --> 00:18:22,890
Will this stick cross a crack?

292
00:18:23,620 --> 00:18:28,130
There's no way to know, just as there's
no way to call a single coin flip.

293
00:18:28,780 --> 00:18:31,780
But many throws together follow a pattern,

294
00:18:31,830 --> 00:18:34,720
and that pattern
is the subject of probability.

295
00:18:35,890 --> 00:18:40,260
Flip a coin again and again,
and keep track of the share of heads.

296
00:18:41,060 --> 00:18:43,010
Early on, it jumps around.

297
00:18:43,490 --> 00:18:46,287
After a thousand flips it hugs one half,

298
00:18:46,313 --> 00:18:49,270
and the longer it runs,
the closer it stays.

299
00:18:50,390 --> 00:18:55,490
Jacob Bernoulli proved this in a book
published in seventeen thirteen: averages

300
00:18:55,540 --> 00:18:57,890
of many random events settle down.

301
00:18:58,730 --> 00:19:00,660
One flip tells you nothing.

302
00:19:00,850 --> 00:19:04,380
A million flips land within
a whisker of one half.

303
00:19:05,470 --> 00:19:09,040
Make the sticks exactly
as long as the boards are wide.

304
00:19:09,370 --> 00:19:13,990
Then describe each throw with two numbers:
how far the middle of the stick

305
00:19:14,040 --> 00:19:17,920
lands from the nearest crack,
and the angle it makes with the boards.

306
00:19:18,860 --> 00:19:20,280
Every throw becomes a dot

307
00:19:20,320 --> 00:19:24,740
in a rectangle of possibilities,
and every dot is equally likely.

308
00:19:25,530 --> 00:19:29,640
The stick crosses a crack exactly
when its dot falls under this curve,

309
00:19:29,720 --> 00:19:31,640
one arch of a sine wave.

310
00:19:32,590 --> 00:19:35,150
So what share of throws should cross?

311
00:19:37,690 --> 00:19:39,970
The share of sticks
that cross is the share

312
00:19:40,020 --> 00:19:42,150
of the rectangle under the arch.

313
00:19:42,810 --> 00:19:47,680
Calculus gives the area under one
arch of a sine wave: exactly two.

314
00:19:48,480 --> 00:19:52,470
The rectangle is a half turn wide,
so its area is pi.

315
00:19:53,470 --> 00:19:58,090
So the crossing share is two over pi,
about sixty-four percent.

316
00:19:59,310 --> 00:20:02,510
And that's where the circle was hiding:
in the angle.

317
00:20:03,060 --> 00:20:07,070
Every direction a stick can point
lies on a circle, and pi is what

318
00:20:07,120 --> 00:20:09,020
it takes to go halfway around.

319
00:20:10,540 --> 00:20:14,660
The French naturalist Buffon worked
this out in the seventeen hundreds.

320
00:20:15,330 --> 00:20:18,050
In nineteen-oh-one,
Mario Lazzarini reported

321
00:20:18,100 --> 00:20:23,160
three thousand four hundred and eight
throws that gave pi to six decimal places.

322
00:20:24,090 --> 00:20:25,930
That's suspiciously good.

323
00:20:26,520 --> 00:20:31,010
A random estimate improves slowly:
each extra correct digit takes about

324
00:20:31,060 --> 00:20:32,910
a hundred times more throws.

325
00:20:33,650 --> 00:20:35,930
His answer was exactly Zu Chongzhi's

326
00:20:35,980 --> 00:20:39,690
fraction, and his numbers
suggest he chose when to stop.

327
00:20:40,740 --> 00:20:44,460
Measuring things with random
samples is now a standard tool,

328
00:20:44,500 --> 00:20:47,650
called the Monte Carlo method,
named in the nineteen forties

329
00:20:47,700 --> 00:20:49,340
after the famous casino.

330
00:20:50,360 --> 00:20:52,910
The animated films you
watch are lit this way:

331
00:20:52,960 --> 00:20:57,470
a computer follows millions of random
paths of light and averages them.

332
00:20:58,400 --> 00:21:01,340
And when a medical trial
reports that a treatment works,

333
00:21:01,380 --> 00:21:05,030
statistics is what tells us whether
the result could just be luck.

334
00:21:06,070 --> 00:21:07,530
Many random events add up

335
00:21:07,580 --> 00:21:12,970
to numbers you can trust, and sometimes
those numbers are old friends, like pi.

336
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:18,433
And to get there,
the sticks needed every branch we've seen.

337
00:21:18,533 --> 00:21:20,620
So let's see how
the branches fit together.

338
00:21:23,640 --> 00:21:24,920
Six turns later,

339
00:21:24,960 --> 00:21:29,460
the map is right back where it started,
just as the group of turns promised.

340
00:21:30,490 --> 00:21:32,980
And the branches
were never really separate.

341
00:21:33,600 --> 00:21:36,110
Statistics runs on calculus.

342
00:21:36,450 --> 00:21:41,483
The secure messages on your phone
run on the algebra of whole numbers.

343
00:21:41,533 --> 00:21:43,050
Physics runs on symmetry.

344
00:21:43,530 --> 00:21:47,220
And under every one of them sit
the definitions we started with.

345
00:21:48,450 --> 00:21:53,890
Back in the chapter on structure,
we promised that e, i and pi would meet.

346
00:21:54,690 --> 00:21:58,770
Turn by pi, a half turn,
and you land on minus one.

347
00:21:59,890 --> 00:22:03,020
Written as an equation,
that's Euler's identity.

348
00:22:04,160 --> 00:22:06,280
The e comes from change.

349
00:22:06,560 --> 00:22:08,380
The i comes from turning.

350
00:22:08,820 --> 00:22:13,590
Pi comes from the circle, and one
and zero are where counting starts.

351
00:22:14,490 --> 00:22:18,030
One short line,
touching almost every part of the map.

352
00:22:19,300 --> 00:22:21,880
And the sticks on the floor
needed all of it.

353
00:22:22,730 --> 00:22:26,210
Definitions said exactly
what counts as a crossing.

354
00:22:26,520 --> 00:22:30,050
Symmetry said every
angle is equally likely.

355
00:22:30,330 --> 00:22:33,980
Geometry turned each throw
into a dot in a rectangle.

356
00:22:34,560 --> 00:22:37,210
Calculus measured the area under the arch.

357
00:22:37,740 --> 00:22:41,260
Probability turned that area
into a share of throws.

358
00:22:41,480 --> 00:22:46,240
And out came pi,
a number no fraction can reach.

359
00:22:47,040 --> 00:22:49,370
No single branch could
have found it alone.

360
00:22:50,700 --> 00:22:54,190
Mathematicians lean
on these connections all the time.

361
00:22:54,570 --> 00:22:57,830
In nineteen ninety-four,
Andrew Wiles settled a question about

362
00:22:57,880 --> 00:23:02,400
whole numbers that had stood open for more
than three hundred and fifty years,

363
00:23:02,440 --> 00:23:05,110
using tools from geometry and algebra.

364
00:23:06,430 --> 00:23:10,360
Every lens saw something true,
and each needed the others.

365
00:23:12,440 --> 00:23:14,520
So, what is a circle?

366
00:23:15,250 --> 00:23:20,560
It's a rule about distance.
It's a length that no fraction can name.

367
00:23:21,010 --> 00:23:26,070
It's every turn at once.
It's a loop that survives any stretch.

368
00:23:26,320 --> 00:23:29,790
It's an area that grows
at the rate of its own edge.

369
00:23:30,080 --> 00:23:33,280
And it's what a floor full
of random sticks was hiding.

370
00:23:34,730 --> 00:23:37,490
Math isn't a pile of separate subjects.

371
00:23:37,880 --> 00:23:42,390
It's one connected landscape,
and every branch is a way into it.

372
00:23:43,580 --> 00:23:48,510
A question to take with you: which branch
felt most like the math you met in school,

373
00:23:48,560 --> 00:23:50,430
and which felt least like it?

374
00:23:52,140 --> 00:23:56,910
To see one of these connections up close,
watch the next video: it takes Euler's

375
00:23:56,960 --> 00:24:00,067
identity apart, piece by piece.

376
00:24:00,453 --> 00:24:04,040
Subscribe so you don't miss it,
and to play with these ideas yourself,

377
00:24:04,080 --> 00:24:08,040
try the interactive app
at I give ideas dot com.
